            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Dust aerosols were also found to have amplified droughts during the US Dust Bowl and Medieval Climate Anomaly (Cook et al., 2008, 2009, 2013), and they affect Atlantic tropical cyclones (e.g., Dunion and Velden, 2004; Wong and Dessler, 2005; Evan et al., 2006; Sun et al., 2008; Strong et al., 2018).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Australia",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "An important aspect of dust activity is its long-term variability, including interannual and decadal variations. Dust emission in North Africa is known to have strong decadal variations (e.g., Prospero and Nees, 1986; Prospero and Lamb, 2003; Mahowald et al., 2010; Evan et al., 2014, 2016), while over Australia, strong interannual variations have been related to El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation (e.g., Marx et al., 2009; Evans et al., 2016).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
